1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,982
Connecting an AI agent to ORCA

2
00:00:03,203 --> 00:00:04,344
is only the beginning.

3
00:00:04,945 --> 00:00:07,948
A useful workflow combines tool access,

4
00:00:08,229 --> 00:00:10,331
scientific method, and evidence.

5
00:00:11,653 --> 00:00:14,096
The slide shows three custom tool wrappers:

6
00:00:14,457 --> 00:00:15,959
prepare an ORCA input,

7
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:19,023
run the calculation, and inspect the output.

8
00:00:19,504 --> 00:00:21,367
They connect the agent to the software.

9
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:23,891
Those names belong to the custom server.

10
00:00:25,093 --> 00:00:26,934
The skill adds the procedure.

11
00:00:27,195 --> 00:00:28,716
It guides valid input

12
00:00:28,816 --> 00:00:30,698
and the choice of electronic state

13
00:00:30,698 --> 00:00:32,039
and calculation settings,

14
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:35,483
including TD-DFT where appropriate.

15
00:00:35,823 --> 00:00:37,265
Then check convergence

16
00:00:37,345 --> 00:00:39,347
and, when the task calls for them,

17
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:40,668
Hessians and spectra.

18
00:00:41,109 --> 00:00:43,431
A result needs scientific interpretation.

19
00:00:44,572 --> 00:00:46,534
Build the workflow around the question.

20
00:00:47,195 --> 00:00:50,278
Load the ORCA skill, prepare the input,

21
00:00:50,679 --> 00:00:53,621
run through MCP, and inspect the output.

22
00:00:54,122 --> 00:00:56,685
Report the settings, evidence, and
limitations.

23
00:00:57,305 --> 00:00:58,927
That lets the researcher focus

24
00:00:58,927 --> 00:01:00,148
on the scientific question

25
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:03,151
while the agent helps manage the calculation.

26
00:01:05,354 --> 00:01:08,181
Make one scientific task into a skill

27
00:01:08,462 --> 00:01:09,704
an agent can follow.

28
00:01:10,606 --> 00:01:12,027
Keep the scope focused.

29
00:01:12,287 --> 00:01:16,649
Geometry optimization, excited-state
calculations, and vibrational

30
00:01:16,649 --> 00:01:18,370
analysis are different tasks.

31
00:01:18,731 --> 00:01:20,791
Build a separate skill for each one,

32
00:01:20,812 --> 00:01:22,652
so the agent knows exactly when to

33
00:01:22,672 --> 00:01:23,073
use it.

34
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:25,754
Put the scientific method in the skill

35
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:29,916
document. Specify the procedure, decision
rules, and

36
00:01:29,916 --> 00:01:32,737
required checks. This tells the agent how

37
00:01:32,737 --> 00:01:35,018
to approach the task, and what evidence

38
00:01:35,018 --> 00:01:36,739
it needs before reporting a result.

39
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,326
Keep supporting material alongside it:
technical references,

40
00:01:41,586 --> 00:01:43,950
example inputs, and reusable scripts.

41
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:45,933
The agent should be able to find

42
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:48,437
the relevant files and prepare the
calculation

43
00:01:48,437 --> 00:01:49,298
without guessing.

44
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:53,683
Then connect execution through an MCP
server.

45
00:01:54,103 --> 00:01:56,185
It lets the agent call ORCA and

46
00:01:56,185 --> 00:01:57,586
retrieve the actual output.

47
00:01:58,186 --> 00:02:00,929
A completed run is not enough: inspect

48
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:03,070
the evidence required by the skill before

49
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:05,592
treating the result as scientifically
credible.

50
00:02:06,753 --> 00:02:08,215
Skills guide the science.

51
00:02:08,715 --> 00:02:10,357
MCP connects the tools.

52
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:11,798
Keep the evidence.

53
00:02:15,162 --> 00:02:16,545
Give scientific skills

54
00:02:16,646 --> 00:02:18,672
a clear home in your project.

55
00:02:19,334 --> 00:02:21,617
Start with a project-local skills folder

56
00:02:21,617 --> 00:02:24,021
in a path your agent actually scans.

57
00:02:24,201 --> 00:02:26,905
The example places it under dot agents,

58
00:02:27,006 --> 00:02:27,907
then skills.

59
00:02:28,128 --> 00:02:29,930
Give each scientific workflow

60
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:30,952
its own folder.

61
00:02:31,974 --> 00:02:33,155
Inside that folder,

62
00:02:33,415 --> 00:02:35,498
the skill file defines the procedure.

63
00:02:35,759 --> 00:02:37,881
References hold syntax and rules.

64
00:02:38,282 --> 00:02:39,964
Assets hold input templates

65
00:02:39,984 --> 00:02:41,306
and molecular coordinates.

66
00:02:41,627 --> 00:02:43,589
Scripts provide validation checks.

67
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:45,031
Keep these supporting files

68
00:02:45,031 --> 00:02:45,872
with the workflow.

69
00:02:46,773 --> 00:02:48,754
That structure supports two goals:

70
00:02:48,994 --> 00:02:51,175
discoverability and isolation.

71
00:02:51,535 --> 00:02:53,136
The agent can find the skill,

72
00:02:53,496 --> 00:02:55,717
while each workflow stays self-contained.

73
00:02:56,138 --> 00:02:58,299
Related workflows can
have separate folders

74
00:02:58,299 --> 00:03:00,039
without mixing their supporting files.

75
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:03,661
Keep the execution integration separate.

76
00:03:03,962 --> 00:03:07,363
Here, the MCP server
provides access to ORCA,

77
00:03:07,643 --> 00:03:10,225
while the skill defines
the scientific procedure.

78
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:11,986
These two roles belong

79
00:03:11,986 --> 00:03:13,726
in separate parts of the project.

80
00:03:14,427 --> 00:03:16,609
Finally, version the instructions,

81
00:03:16,609 --> 00:03:17,810
templates, and checks.

82
00:03:17,951 --> 00:03:19,892
Keep large simulation outputs

83
00:03:19,893 --> 00:03:21,194
out of that repository.

84
00:03:21,434 --> 00:03:23,536
A clear structure makes the library

85
00:03:23,536 --> 00:03:26,759
reusable, maintainable,
and easier to audit.

86
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:33,659
A useful skill needs clear organization separate the

87
00:03:33,659 --> 00:03:36,379
instructions from supporting material so a

88
00:03:36,379 --> 00:03:39,539
scientific workflow is easier to understand reuse and

89
00:03:39,539 --> 00:03:39,919
check.

90
00:03:41,059 --> 00:03:42,499
Start with the main skill file.

91
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:43,039
It

92
00:03:43,039 --> 00:03:45,979
explains when to use the skill, which steps

93
00:03:45,979 --> 00:03:48,139
to follow and what must be checked.

94
00:03:48,659 --> 00:03:49,599
Keep that operating

95
00:03:49,599 --> 00:03:50,819
procedure concise.

96
00:03:51,899 --> 00:03:55,119
The references folder holds focused supporting knowledge.

97
00:03:55,459 --> 00:03:56,539
In this ORCA example,

98
00:03:56,539 --> 00:04:00,399
that includes input syntax, calculation methods, and validation

99
00:04:00,399 --> 00:04:01,039
guidance.

100
00:04:01,579 --> 00:04:02,819
Load the relevant detail

101
00:04:02,819 --> 00:04:04,439
when the task needs it.

102
00:04:04,859 --> 00:04:08,659
The assets folder stores reusable starting material, input

103
00:04:08,659 --> 00:04:09,259
templates,

104
00:04:09,599 --> 00:04:11,879
molecular coordinates, and example files.

105
00:04:12,819 --> 00:04:15,939
Label examples clearly, and adapt their settings to

106
00:04:15,939 --> 00:04:17,079
the actual calculation.

107
00:04:18,019 --> 00:04:21,739
The scripts folder turns repeatable checks into executable

108
00:04:21,739 --> 00:04:22,279
steps.

109
00:04:22,919 --> 00:04:23,199
A script

110
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:26,559
can inspect an input file, extract real results,

111
00:04:26,759 --> 00:04:28,479
or report a specific error.

112
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:30,099
Check failures before

113
00:04:30,099 --> 00:04:30,459
proceed.

114
00:04:31,059 --> 00:04:33,499
Only the main skill file is required.

115
00:04:33,839 --> 00:04:36,079
The supporting folders are optional, keep

116
00:04:36,079 --> 00:04:40,259
instructions concise, organize detailed knowledge separately, and automate

117
00:04:40,259 --> 00:04:41,419
checks where practical

118
00:04:41,419 --> 00:04:43,599
that makes the workflow easier to maintain and

119
00:04:43,599 --> 00:04:43,779
review.

120
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:48,731
A scientific skill file guides decisions

121
00:04:48,952 --> 00:04:50,578
and requires evidence.

122
00:04:51,321 --> 00:04:53,584
Start with the name and description.

123
00:04:54,005 --> 00:04:56,229
They tell the agent what the skill does

124
00:04:56,510 --> 00:04:57,812
and when to load it.

125
00:04:58,374 --> 00:05:01,158
Keep that purpose clear and specific.

126
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:04,581
Next, define what must be known

127
00:05:04,581 --> 00:05:06,042
before the calculation.

128
00:05:06,122 --> 00:05:07,742
In this chemistry example,

129
00:05:08,043 --> 00:05:11,084
require the molecular geometry, charge,

130
00:05:11,084 --> 00:05:12,564
spin multiplicity,

131
00:05:12,825 --> 00:05:15,105
and number of excited states.

132
00:05:15,386 --> 00:05:17,166
Ask for missing information.

133
00:05:18,287 --> 00:05:20,250
Then write the workflow in order.

134
00:05:20,670 --> 00:05:22,452
Consult the syntax reference,

135
00:05:22,733 --> 00:05:23,894
prepare the input,

136
00:05:24,054 --> 00:05:25,456
and run the input checker.

137
00:05:25,717 --> 00:05:27,839
In this example, obtain approval

138
00:05:28,019 --> 00:05:30,802
before invoking the ORCA tool through MCP.

139
00:05:31,243 --> 00:05:33,686
Inspect the real output before reporting.

140
00:05:34,627 --> 00:05:36,909
Finally, define the evidence rules.

141
00:05:37,369 --> 00:05:39,751
Specify the completion
and scientific checks

142
00:05:39,751 --> 00:05:41,072
required for a result.

143
00:05:41,412 --> 00:05:43,354
Report what the output supports,

144
00:05:43,414 --> 00:05:44,895
without inventing spectra

145
00:05:44,915 --> 00:05:46,917
or claiming unverified success.

146
00:05:47,217 --> 00:05:49,179
Clear decision gates make the workflow

147
00:05:49,179 --> 00:05:50,940
more disciplined and reproducible.

148
00:05:54,041 --> 00:05:57,144
Give your AI focused references it can

149
00:05:57,164 --> 00:05:58,065
check and use.

150
00:05:59,207 --> 00:06:01,209
Keep the skill document focused on the

151
00:06:01,209 --> 00:06:05,234
workflow. Move detailed documentation into
small reference

152
00:06:05,234 --> 00:06:08,658
files: one for input syntax, another for

153
00:06:08,658 --> 00:06:11,862
method selection, and another for
validation checks.

154
00:06:12,363 --> 00:06:14,605
That makes the relevant guidance easier to

155
00:06:14,605 --> 00:06:14,986
find.

156
00:06:16,087 --> 00:06:18,310
Preserve links to the official manual and

157
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:19,872
identify the software version.

158
00:06:20,293 --> 00:06:22,716
Each reference should explain the valid
syntax,

159
00:06:22,997 --> 00:06:25,240
the prerequisites, and the limitations.

160
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:28,144
The original documentation remains the
place to

161
00:06:28,144 --> 00:06:29,505
verify the details.

162
00:06:30,367 --> 00:06:33,568
For example, consider percent max core one

163
00:06:33,568 --> 00:06:36,489
thousand. In ORCA, this sets roughly one

164
00:06:36,489 --> 00:06:39,310
thousand megabytes per processing core for
the

165
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:40,710
dominant scratch arrays.

166
00:06:40,990 --> 00:06:42,291
It is not a limit on the

167
00:06:42,291 --> 00:06:43,931
entire calculation's memory.

168
00:06:44,391 --> 00:06:46,672
Other allocations can add to that amount,

169
00:06:46,912 --> 00:06:49,613
so parallel jobs need careful memory
planning.

170
00:06:50,894 --> 00:06:52,497
Keep instructions focused.

171
00:06:52,838 --> 00:06:56,124
Make the supporting details precise,
traceable, and

172
00:06:56,144 --> 00:06:56,604
usable.

173
00:06:58,968 --> 00:07:02,855
A reusable ORCA skill needs more than
instructions.

174
00:07:03,357 --> 00:07:08,126
Three support folders make the workflow easier
to maintain.

175
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:12,150
References hold version-specific guidance.

176
00:07:12,551 --> 00:07:15,574
The example documents ORCA input syntax

177
00:07:15,594 --> 00:07:17,876
and points back to the official manual.

178
00:07:18,397 --> 00:07:21,020
That gives the agent a source to consult.

179
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:24,623
Assets provide a reusable starting point.

180
00:07:25,064 --> 00:07:28,727
In this example, the template requests ten
excited states.

181
00:07:29,008 --> 00:07:31,090
It reads a separate geometry file.

182
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:33,632
Adapt the settings to the scientific question.

183
00:07:34,794 --> 00:07:37,497
Scripts perform repeatable input checks.

184
00:07:37,817 --> 00:07:40,180
The example checks keywords, blocks,

185
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:42,021
and whether the geometry file exists.

186
00:07:42,382 --> 00:07:44,844
It reports passes, warnings, or errors.

187
00:07:45,185 --> 00:07:47,147
It does not run ORCA.

188
00:07:48,047 --> 00:07:50,489
Keep the three support roles distinct.

189
00:07:50,489 --> 00:07:53,752
References explain. Assets provide templates.

190
00:07:54,052 --> 00:07:55,453
Scripts check the input.

191
00:07:55,554 --> 00:07:58,176
A passed check does not prove scientific
accuracy.

192
00:07:58,516 --> 00:08:01,198
Run ORCA, then evaluate the output

193
00:08:01,198 --> 00:08:02,680
before trusting the result.

194
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,720
Build a workflow you can test and review.

195
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:10,420
Start with one working ORCA skill.

196
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:12,080
Then connect it to

197
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:15,140
the tools needed for a specific scientific task.

198
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:18,600
First place the skill and its supporting files

199
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:20,020
inside your project.

200
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:23,160
Then run the python input checker on the

201
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:24,040
example input.

202
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:25,680
This is an offline

203
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:26,700
